# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-induction.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; hume-induction.logic.r2.2 has "no_contradiction → ¬arg_demo"; they differ when only "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" and "there is a demonstrative argument, concerning relations of ideas, for trusting past experience" are true, which the first allows
hume-induction.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" and "there is a demonstrative argument, concerning relations of ideas, for trusting past experience" are true. Is S2, "That there are no demonstrative arguments in the case seems evident; since it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change, and that an object, seemingly like those which we have experienced, may be attended with different or contrary effects.", true then?
hume-induction.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 3 ways: hume-induction.logic.r2 has "nature_change_conceivable → no_contradiction ∧ ¬arg_demo"; hume-induction.logic.r2.2 has "□(nature_change_conceivable → no_contradiction)"; they differ when only "that the course of nature may change, and an object like those experienced be attended with different or contrary effects, can be clearly and distinctly conceived" and "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" are true, which the first allows; hume-induction.logic.r2.3 has "□(nature_change_conceivable → no_contradiction)" and "no_contradiction → ¬arg_demo"
hume-induction.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S5 is read hume-induction.logic.r2 with no modality; hume-induction.logic.r2.2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "can" does not say which it means
hume-induction.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r2.2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "that the course of nature may change, and an object like those experienced be attended with different or contrary effects, can be clearly and distinctly conceived" and "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" are true and "there is a demonstrative argument, concerning relations of ideas, for trusting past experience", "necessarily, (if that the course of nature may change, and an object like those experienced be attended with different or contrary effects, can be clearly and distinctly conceived, then it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change)" and "necessarily, (if that the course of nature may change, and an object like those experienced be attended with different or contrary effects, can be clearly and distinctly conceived, then it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change)" are false. Is S5, "Now whatever is intelligible, and can be distinctly conceived, implies no contradiction, and can never be proved false by any demonstrative argument or abstract reasoning à priori.", true then?
hume-induction.md:3: argue/readings: S9 is read 3 ways: hume-induction.logic.r2 has "founded_cause ∧ cause_from_experience ∧ presuppose_uniformity → (arg_moral → circular)"; hume-induction.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "there is a probable argument, regarding matter of fact and existence, that proves that the future will be conformable to the past", "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect", "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true, which the second allows; hume-induction.logic.r2.3 has "founded_cause ∧ cause_from_experience ∧ presuppose_uniformity → circular"
hume-induction.md:3: argue/answered: S9: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "there is a probable argument, regarding matter of fact and existence, that proves that the future will be conformable to the past", "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect", "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true and "proving the supposition by probable arguments is going in a circle, taking for granted the very point in question" is false. Is S9, "To endeavour, therefore, the proof of this last supposition by probable arguments, or arguments regarding existence, must be evidently going in a circle, and taking that for granted, which is the very point in question.", true then?
hume-induction.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.3 have "circular → ¬arg_moral"; hume-induction.logic.r2.2 has "founded_cause ∧ cause_from_experience ∧ presuppose_uniformity → circular" and "circular → ¬arg_moral"; they differ when only "there is a probable argument, regarding matter of fact and existence, that proves that the future will be conformable to the past", "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect", "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true, which the first allows
hume-induction.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: hume-induction.logic.r2.2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect and our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience and all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past), then proving the supposition by probable arguments is going in a circle, taking for granted the very point in question"; hume-induction.logic.r2 does not
hume-induction.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r2.2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect and our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience and all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past), then proving the supposition by probable arguments is going in a circle, taking for granted the very point in question"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
hume-induction.md:1: argue/unfaithful: hume-induction.logic.r2 P1 (S1): the sentence has no word of "there are arguments which engage us to put trust in past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement": is it the sentence P1 came from?
hume-induction.md:1: argue/negation: hume-induction.logic.r2 P2 (S3): the sentence says "not", but P2 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "that the course of nature may change, and an object like those experienced be attended with different or contrary effects, can be clearly and distinctly conceived"
